    {
      "id": "b385ae51-cfde-4f3a-8dc3-2e8c0584643d",
      "phaseId": "21cc78bb-e0d6-4b57-a20c-a18cb98cd215",
      "number": 306,
      "shortId": "GQHXR",
      "priority": 5,
      "shortName": "scrollback-search-migrate-the",
      "title": "Scrollback search: migrate the bar to widgets, make search per-pane, use a real Input",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "═══ DONE 2026-07-25 — PR #246, branch `fix/search-bar-widgets` ═══\nUser-verified in the running app at each step. Filed in THIS phase because theming-05 was closed as done while the scrollback-search bar was still hand-drawn — it was the item the phase missed.\n\nHOW IT SURFACED: after F003/P011/T016 the user asked \"check if we can start replacing widgets in the app with the new ones\". The survey found the app has NO app-side `drop_shadow`/`bracket_frame` and no hand-rolled placement — it already composes library widgets — with exactly one exception: `paint_search_bar` in `heca/src/chrome/mod.rs`.\n\n═══ 1. THE BAR WAS HAND-DRAWN ═══\nIt estimated its own width as `chars * font * 0.62` (a hardcoded glyph advance ratio), clamped to a hand-picked `120..480`, positioned itself by arithmetic (`x = px + pw - bar_w - inset`), and used literal `pad = 8.0`, `inset = 8.0`, `with_alpha(200)`, `SEARCH_BAR_FONT = 13.0`.\nNOW: a query field + segmented `Tag` inside a `Flex` sized to the pane, positioned by `justify(End)`/`align(End)` + margins. The engine measures and positions; nothing here measures text. `SEARCH_BAR_FONT` deleted — the tree inherits the chrome font via `LayoutEngine::base_font`.\nDELIBERATELY LEFT AS PAINTED PRIMITIVES: the bell flash and the per-cell match highlights track terminal CONTENT, not the widget tree; a widget per match would be absurd. Their one literal (`2.0` radius) now reads `theme.colors.control_radius()`. The boundary is written into docs/widgets.md.\n\n═══ 2. SEARCH IS NOW PER PANE ═══\n`AppState.search: Option<SearchState>` was ONE slot for the whole app, so starting a search in a second pane silently destroyed the first — bar and highlights vanished with no way back (user: \"doing more search cause other panes to disappear\"). Now `searches: BTreeMap<PaneId, SearchState>`; every pane with a search draws its own.\n- `SearchState::pane_id` REMOVED — the map key is the single source of truth, so the two cannot disagree.\n- `BTreeMap` + `Ord` on `PaneId` (heca-core/src/layout/types.rs) rather than a hash map: these are DRAWN, so iteration order must be stable frame to frame.\n- ⚠️ `AppState::search_target_pane()` is the ONE definition of which pane a keystroke affects (the selection's pane if copy-mode owns one, else the focused pane). LOAD-BEARING: `enter_scrollback_search` picks its pane from the SELECTION, so an edit path keyed on `focused_pane` writes to an entry that does not exist whenever the two differ — query frozen, no error. The old single-slot state hid this by carrying its own `pane_id`.\n- Pane close drops its search (`clear_search`) at every removal site including bulk removal.\n\n═══ 3. THE QUERY IS A REAL `Input` ═══\nIt was a bare `String` driven by a hand-written handler that understood Escape/Enter/Backspace/one char — so `Ctrl+u`, `Ctrl+w`, `Ctrl+a`, `Alt+Backspace`, caret motion and selection silently did NOTHING here while working in every other field (user: \"the input does not respect the rules and behavior we have for inputs... we have to use our widgets\").\nNOTHING NEEDED WRITING: `Input` owns the editing model and the host `widget_keymap` already resolves the chords (`combo_to_grid` → `Keymap::dispatch` → `[keys.widgets]`). `handle_search_mode` now routes through that same path; Esc/Enter stay mode transitions handled before the field sees them.\nRENDERING: a field must be RETAINED to keep its caret across frames, so it cannot be moved into the per-frame tree. The tree reserves a slot sized to what the engine measured and the retained field is painted into it — the arrangement `CommandPalette` already uses for its own query line.\n\n═══ 4. ENTER WAS A DEAD END (three separate causes) ═══\n- `/` on a pane that already had a search INSERTED A NEW STATE, wiping the query. Now resumes.\n- Clicking the field did nothing: the bar is painted onto the chrome scene and never took part in hit-testing. `mouse.rs::search_field_press` now focuses it, re-enters query entry, and hands the press to the `Input` so it places the caret itself. Checked BEFORE the content-pane handlers so a press on the floating bar is not also read as a click into the terminal underneath.\n- The field kept `focused` after Enter — a caret shown while no longer taking keys.\n\n═══ 5. LAYOUT ENGINE BUG, FIXED AT SOURCE ═══\n`LayoutEngine::compute` assigned the root at `(0,0)` and SILENTLY DROPPED a margin set on it. `.margin_left(x)` worked on every child and did nothing on a root, with no error and nothing in the API to suggest a difference. The search bar (a pane-sized box margined to the pane's origin) therefore drew at the window's corner — over the SIDEBAR, a whole pane from its terminal.\nI first worked around it with a viewport-sized wrapper plus a ⚠️ note in docs/widgets.md. The user pushed back — correctly — that a warning nobody will read is not a fix, and that nobody would ever have noticed it. `compute` now offsets the root by its own margin; the wrapper and the note are GONE. Verified no other `compute` call site passes a root with a margin, so nothing else moved.\n\n═══ 6. KEYBINDING DOCS (e1d14c8) ═══\n`trigger = \"\"` was used by the built-in `selection` mode and documented NOWHERE. Worse, keybindings.default.toml claimed redefining a built-in mode \"only extends/overrides its bindings\" — untrue in a way that silently breaks keys: `bindings` are ADDED but `trigger`/`sticky` are REPLACED (`build_modes`, heca/src/app/registry.rs:306-314). Copying the `resize` block to add one key, minus the `trigger` line, stops `prefix+r` entering it with nothing to explain why. Fixed in keybindings.default.toml + README with a worked example, plus the fact that `[keys]` works from `config.toml` too (both files deep-merge before parsing).\n\n═══ TESTS ═══\nWorkspace 896 → 899 green; clippy clean under `--all-targets --all-features --locked -- -D warnings`.\n- `layout::tests::` — `the_root_is_offset_by_its_own_margin`, `a_root_margin_moves_the_whole_subtree`, `a_root_without_a_margin_starts_at_the_origin`.\n- `chrome::search_bar_tests::` — lands inside its pane, hugs the bottom-right, a wider field makes a wider bar, the reserved slot matches the field size. VERIFIED THESE BITE by reintroducing the root-margin bug: they fail, reporting x=524 against a pane at x=600.\n\n═══ NOT DONE — REAL GAP ═══\nThe per-pane behaviour itself is NOT unit-tested. It needs an `AppState`, which owns a `wgpu::Surface<'static>`, `Device`, `Queue` and four renderers (77 fields); a surface needs a real window, so it cannot be built in a test. I proposed a fixture, the user approved, I hit that blocker and — instead of reporting it — extracted a `Searches` type and committed it. The user asked for that to be reverted and it was (never pushed). If this area is to be tested the approach must be agreed FIRST; the plausible route is making the surface optional across the render path so a headless `AppState` is constructible, which is substantial and belongs in its own task.\n\n═══ PROCESS NOTE (user-raised, repeatedly) ═══\n\"do not hardcode. try to reuse existing pattern and widgets. But you keep failing all the time.\" Both the hand-rolled key handler here and the ring-of-copies glow in T016 were written before looking for the thing that already did the job (`Input` + `widget_keymap`; the placement/chrome authorities). The check that matters: SEARCH FIRST, then write. And a caveat written about existing behaviour means a defect was found — the only allowed outcomes are fix it now or file a task, never prose. This task exists because of that rule.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\nDelivered on branch `fix/search-bar-widgets` → PR #246. Commits: search bar rebuilt from widgets; per-pane search; `Input` query field; Enter/click re-entry; `e1d14c8` keybinding docs; `4666aaa` `LayoutEngine::compute` root-margin fix.\n\nWorkspace 899 tests green, clippy clean under `--all-targets --all-features --locked -- -D warnings`. Every visual step user-verified in the running app.\n\nCarries one recorded gap: the per-pane behaviour has no unit test because `AppState` cannot be constructed in one (it owns a `wgpu::Surface<'static>` and a surface needs a real window). Making that testable is a separate task whose approach must be agreed before it is started.",
      "notes": "",
